Goals:
-----

 - Develop a treatment of CPU and GPU versions of the same code that:
 
     1. Allows maintenance of a single definition of each class used
     on both CPU and GPU, whenever possible.

     2. Avoiding the need to create parallel sets of classes
     (or trivial subclasses) in the Rpc and Rpg namespaces.

     3. Makes efficient use of existing code when possible - avoid the
     need to start over.

     4. Create a manageable build system that allows for maintaining 
     object files for several compiled versions of the code. Ideally, 
     I'd like to be able to maintain several versions simultaneously.

     5. Create a manageable system for unit tests, with little or no
     code duplication in the tests.

Comments: Goal 1 has been largely accomplished b current system of class
templates in namespace Rp, using "Types" classes as template parameters
that encapsulate collections of type aliases.  This file proposes a way 
to accomplish goal 2, and avoid the need for distinct Rpc and Rpg 
namespaces.  

Discussion
----------

The most basic problem in maintaining CPU and GPU versions of the same
code arises from the need to create CPU and GPU versions of the classes
that store fields that are defined on the nodes of a mesh.  All or almost 
all of the data that is stored on the GPU in the current cuda version are
fields of this type.  Relevant mathematical operations include things 
like binary and in-place (compound) arithmetic operations, exponentiation, 
and FFTs. 

Strategies for specializing the behavior for CPUs and GPUs can be based
on a system that differentiates either at run time (via inheritance and 
polymorphic virtual functions) or at compile time (via templates).

A. Run time polymorphism via inheritance and virtual functions:

Strategy: Create an interface / base class for each field class, and define
subclasses designed to use CPU and GPU memory.  Run-time polymorphism must 
rely on the use of virtual functions to distinguish behavior, and so requires 
that mathematical operation that are performed on a field all be implemented 
by virtual functions.

Comments:

   - In virtual functions that implement operations for which the output
     is a field, and one or more other fields may be provided as input
     parameters, it is natural to call the member function on the field 
     that will hold the output, since there is always one output, but
     there may be any number of inputs.

   - To create generic code, functions that take fields as parameters
     must take references to base classes as parameters. Operations are 
     actually only allowed for input and output fields that are defined
     on the same grid and that use the same "backend".  There thus must 
     be a run-time check of some sort of label to ensure that all fields 
     involved in an operation are compatible in this sense. Because both
     mesh dimensions and backend must be compatible, it is natural to 
     create a structure that encapsulates the attributes and checks them
     together. 

B. Compile time polymorphism via templates.

This is the proposal that is outlined in the remainder of this file. In 
this proposal, we define class templates that take a template parameter 
that uniquely identifies the backend. 

Specifically, to take advantage of analogies with the existing 
implementation in namespace Pscf::Rp, I propose to define many classes 
that each take two template parameters: 

   -  The integer dimension of space D=1,2, or 3, and
   -  A class or enumeration that is uniquely identified with a 
     particular backend. 

I tentatively propose to use a classes to identify the backend because 
a classes can contain additional information, e.g., about associated 
types used by the associated FFT library), and because this is most
similar to the existing system. 

In this approach, we would define a base class template for RField as

template <int D, class T> 
class Template {};

and then define partial specializations in which T is taken to be 
either T=CpuTypes for CPU code or T=CudaTypes for GPU code written in 
Cuda. 

Class templates with the same two template parameters would also be 
written for a variety of other higher level classes that own or interact
with field classes. A class template Parent<D,T> for a parent class that
owns an RField can then have a member Field<D,T>. In this manner we can
create a hierarchy of templates that own each other, such that all of
the templates used in a given executable would have the same values for
both D and T.

Comments:

  - Mathematical operations can be implemented as global functions
    (for vector operations and reduction operations) or as member 
    functions of an associated FFT<D,T> class for FFT operations, 
    exactly as done currently. It appears that no changes would need 
    to be made to any of the existing mathematical functions. Continued
    use of global functions as makes it easy to add new mathematical 
    operations when convenient without adding additional virtual 
    functions to the field classes.

  - The check that all fields or arrays involved in a mathematical 
    operations must use the same backend would be automatically imposed 
    by the C++ type system, rather than as run-time-checks: The data 
    types for fields are specializations of templates in which one 
    of the template parmeters identifies the backend, so the backend 
    is embedded in the type definitions. Functions would we defined 
    for allowed operations, involving fields that use the same backend, 
    but not for prohibited operations. The compiler will choose the 
    correct version of overloaded functions when instantiating each
    class template specialization. 
    
  - I need to think about how to implement unit tests, but it may
    be natural to define some of these as member function templates
    that take the backend types class T as a template argument. This
    part is not fully fleshed out.

Thoughts:

  - The template approach is arguably a more natural conceptual fit to 
    the software design.  In either design, a single backend will always 
    be used throughout execution of a given program, so the backend for 
    the entire program can be specified at compile time, as a template 
    parameter.  The flexibility promised by a system based on run-time 
    polymorphism simply isn't necessary, or ever used: We dont' use or 
    need the freedom to choose which backend to use during program 
    execution, or to allow for the creation of objects with different 
    backends during execution. Interfaces required by an inheritance
    based solution imply that objects with different backends can be 
    used interchangeably, but this is not true: All the objects in a 
    given program must use the same backend for the program to function.

  - I think that the strongest argument for preferring compile-time 
    polymorhpisms really has to do with the strengths and defects of
    the C++ language: The C++ mechanisms for working with templates in 
    a large code base are simply much clumsier than mechanisms for 
    working with a system based on inheritances. The awkwardness of 
    C++ mechanisms for working with files that define templates is
    largely a result of the use of a text preprocessor to literally 
    cut-and-paste header files as a way of importing interface 
    information that C++ inherited from C, and the way that old 
    (archaic?) mechanism interacts with C++ templates.  The added 
    complexity required to work with templates in C++ is, however, a 
    real consideration, and provides an important reason to consider
    a solution based on run-time polymorphism even for problems in 
    which compile-time polymorphism is a reasonable option.

  - The current version of PSCF uses a system of templates that is 
    in some ways very similar to that proposed here. In the current
    system, the Rp namespace already contains a set of classes that
    almost all have two class template templates analogous to the two 
    proposed here.  In the process of developing the current 
    template-based system, we have already developed methods to avoid 
    some of the potential problems of large-template based frameworks 
    in C++ (e.g., potentially long compilation times and mysterious 
    error) by systematic use of explicit instantiation of all required 
    template specializations.

  - The key difference between the current and proposed systems is that
    the proposed system would remove the need for the existence of 
    formally distinct CPU and GPU versions of each class that exist in
    different namespace. Currently for a class named "Class", there is
    generally a template class template Rp::Class<D,T> that defines all
    or most of the class definition, and subclasses Rpc::Class<D> and
    Rpg::Class<D> that are derived from specializations of Rp::Class
    with different values for the Types class. The subclasses defined
    in Rpc and Rpg are often trivial subclasses, with little or no
    new code, which are required simply in order to segregate names 
    for Cpu and Gpu code. In the new system, we would work directly 
    with specializations of Rp::Class<D,T> with different values for
    the types class T= CpuTypes or T=CudaTypes. 

  - By getting rid of the Rpc and Rpg namespaces, we would get rid of
    the need for the Types classes to maintain sets of aliases that
    define generic names that refer to classes defined in the Rpc and
    Rpg namespaces. The Types classes definitions would become much
    smaller, and would contain only a few types definitions for types
    used by the FFT classes.

  - IMHO, the ugliest aspects of the current system is the need to 
    define separate completely parallel sets of classes in Rpc and Rpg
    namespaces, even when analogous classes derive all or almost all of
    their source code, and the need to use giant lists of aliases in
    the Types classes. This proposal is designed to fix that, while
    continuing to use a template parameter to identify a backend at
    compile time.

  - In a system in which most classes take two template parameters
    (dimension of space D, and a second parameter T that acts as an
    identifier for a backend (cpu, cuda, openmp, hip, etc.), I can
    see two reasonable options for the nature of the second template
    parameter:

      A. It can be a class, as described above. One advantage of 
      this is that this provides a simple syntax for accessing
      associated information about associated types or other 
      info, via "typename T::Complex" or "T:: backend", etc.

      B. The alternative is to make the second parameter an 
      enum value (type Backend) and then use a Traits class 
      Traits<T>::Complex to access related information. 

    Either would work fine. The main reason that I curently prefer
    A (use of a class) is that it is more similar to the current 
    system and provides an easier path for conversion (discussed 
    below) by a method that preserves working, testable code at 
    intermediate steps.  After finishing a conversion to design A, 
    it would be relatively easy to convert to design B in a separate 
    step, by globally converting "T::" to "Traits<T>::", if I thought 
    the additional change was worth the trouble.

  - As already noted, the proposed template based proposal would allow 
    re-use of all of the existing code for basic mathematical operations, 
    without modification. As discussed below, it also provides a path 
    to an improved design by stepwise changes, without ever breaking 
    the code. It appears to me that a change to an inheritance based
    design would require a more drastic rewrite.

  - The template-based system proposed here might or might not have
    been the best option if we were designing a new package from
    scratch. I would see an argument for the inheritance based system 
    if I were starting over, mostly because I think it is somewhat easier 
    for grad student developers to understand an inheritance based system
    than a template based system. The proposed template-based design is
    a logically reasonable option that provides an easier path for 
    converting from the current system, of their similarity .

