Plan (order of operations)
-----------------------------

Stage I: (non-disruptive preparatory change):

1)  Change the few classes in Rp that use several explicit class template 
    parameters rather than a Types class so as to use a Types class 
    template parameter. This will enable the transformation of the role 
    of the Types class from a list of aliases to (primarily) an identifier 
    for a backend.

Stage II: (Unify templates that now require explicit specialization)

These are steps that could be taken either before or after more
disruptive changes described below:

1)  Make the FftwDArray class from which RField, RFieldDft and CField 
    are derived behave like a DRArray, so that it can be referred to by
    other objects of the same type, with reference counting to ensure
    memory safety.  This will make  it is possible to create a zero-cost 
    reference to a CPU field to create a structure in CPU code analogous 
    to the use of distinct device and host arrays in GPU code: In Cpu 
    code a "HostArray" could simply be a reference to a Field container,
    by sharing a pointer, rather than an actual deep copy.  (Done)

2)  Create aliases named HostArray, HostRField, HostRFieldDft and 
    HostCField in the Types classes. In the CPU version, make these 
    specializations of FftwDArray, so that they can be used as references.

3)  Develop an abstraction to treat device <-> copy operations that are
    required for some Gpu implementations but not for the analogous CPU 
    implementations, in order to further reduce the number of functions 
    that need different implementations for Cpu and Gpu code. The CPU
    implementations should create a reference rather than a copy, and
    the GPU implementations should copy data between host and device.

4)  More generally, work on reducing the number of class templates in Rp 
    which require subclasses that need to implement virtual functions in 
    Rpc and Rpg namespaces to specialize behavior.  The less inheritance 
    is used to specialize CPU and GPU algorithms in the existing code, 
    the easier it will be to transform to a unitary template based design.

Stage III: (Converting subclasses in Rpc and Rpg to specializations)

    Goal: Get rid of all subclasses in the Rpc and Rpg namespace, and
    replace them by specializations of class templates defined in the 
    Rp namespace.

    The required procedure is different for:

       - trivial subclasses (with no overridden functions) 
       - nontrivial subclasses (with overridden fucntions)

    For each class template in Rp with trivial subclasses in Rpc and Rpg:

       - Throughout the Rp namespace, globally substitute:

             "typename T::Class" => Class<D,T>

       - Remove the associating "using" statement from both Types classes

       - Remove the associated forward declaration from both Types classes

       - Put forward declarations in class template headers as needed

       - Remove definitions and explicit instantiation of associated 
         subclasses in Rpc and Rpg

    For each class template in Rp with non-trivial subclasses:

       - Change name of base class template by adding "Base" to name

       - Define a primary template for Class without "Base" suffix

       - Change subcclasses to specializations, and move to Rp namespace

       - Then go through remaining steps as for trivial subclasses (above)

    At the end of this process, the Types classes should not contain any
    remaining aliases for classes defined in the Rpc and Rpg namespaces.

    After conversion of all classes in Rpc and Rpg namespaces, the only
    remaining classes in these namespaces should be the Types classes.

Stage IV (Change location and role of Types classes):

1)  Create Prdc::CpuTypes<D> by copying Pscf::Rpc::Types and create 
    Prdc::CudaTypes<D> by copying Pscf::Rpg::Types. Please these in
    prdc/field/cpu and prdc/field/cuda. These may still contain 
    using statements for field and FFT classes.

2)  Define a enumeration that identifies backends and make a value of it
    a static member of the existing Types classes. This would allow code 
    that takes a Types class as a template argument to ask explicitly at
    run time what type of backend is being used, and construct some logic
    that can execute different paths for different back ends. Please this
    in prdc/field.

3)  Add Complex and Real type aliases to these new Types classes, to 
    represent the data types used by associated FFT classes. 

4)  Change references to Rpc::Types to CpuTypes and Rpg::Types to 
    CudaTypes, respectively, throughout remaining files in the rpc/
    and rpg/ directories. Also change file names for the corresponding
    header file includes.

Stage V (Convert Field and FFT classes to two-parameter templates)

1)  Create templates for RField, RFieldDft, CField etc. in Prdc

    - Create directories prdc/field/cpu and prdc/field/cuda in
      which to place backend-specific field and FFT classes

    - Create empty primary class templates in Prdc (prdc/field) of the 
      form class RField<int D, class T> where T is a "*Types" class,
      and similarly for RFieldDft, CField, FFT, etc. Include
      declarations of explicit specializations for this.

    - Make explicit partial specializations of RField, RFieldDft,
      CField, FFT, etc. that are just renamed versions of existing 
      classes now in Prdc::Cpu and Prdc::Cuda. Each of these
      partial specializations will still have D as an unspecified
      template parameter, like the existing classes.

      For example: 

         Prdc::Cpu::RField<int D> => Prdc::RField<int D, CpuTypes>
 
      Idea: Use template parameter rather than Cpu and Cuda namespace 
      qualifiers to distinguish names of Cpu and Gpu variants of 
      analogous field and FFT classes.  

    - Advantage: Template parameters in the class template of a 
      parent object can be used in type definitions for member
      variables and function interfaces.  Note: Namespaces cannot 
      be used as template parameters, so we have to use a class or
      enumeration as a template parameter to identify a backend.

    - Note: No change should be necessary to functions defined 
      in VecOp and Reduce. These functions operate on DArray or 
      DeviceArray containers, which are base classes for the CPU 
      and GPU field containers, respectively.

2)  Make any other required changes in Prdc namespace to get this 
    namespace level directory to compile. Look for direct reference 
    to, e.g., RField<D> and FFT<D> template specializations and 
    replace these by RField<D,T> and FFT<D,T> etc.

3)  Repeat change in class template names for definitions of all 
    prdc unit tests. At this point code and unit tests in src/prdc 
    should compiles and run successfully. 

4)  Change aliases to Fields and FFT types in CpuTypes and CudaTypes. 
    This will immediately allow re-use throughout the class templates 
    in Rp.

5)  In Rp namespace, replace references to aliases such T::RFields 
    by explicit template specializations, RFields<D,T>, etc. This
    will get rid of the remaining aliases.

6)  Find  and replace direct references to, e.g., RField<D> and FFT<D> 
    type names in Rpc and Rpg directories with Field<D,CpuTypes> and
    FFT<D,CpuTypes>, etc.

    (Code in rpc and rpg should compiles again at this point, but not 
    unit tests)

7)  Find and replace direct references to RField<D>, FFT<D>, etc. 
    in rpc and rpg unit tests with, references to Field<D,CpuTypes> ,
    FFT<D,CpuTypes>, etc.

    (All code and unit tests should again compile and run)

    At this point, the rpc/ and rpg/ namespaces will contain *.cpp or
    *. cu files, respectively, but no headers. Each *.cpp file or *.cu
    file will contain just a set of explicit instantiation directives,
    all of which can be instantiated using one or two macros.  The rpc/ 
    and rpg/ namespaces will thus essentially have been converted into 
    separate build directories for CPU and GPU code, to allow both 
    versions to be built simultaneously. This will work for the moment,
    but can later be converted to a single build directory by changing
    pattern rules for compilation.

Stage VI: Change build system, move files to src/rp

1) Add build system files (patterns.mk, sources.mk, makefile) to
rp directory

2) Modify pattern rules to allow creation of object files with different
suffixes created from source files with different suffixes. Recommendation:

    *.o   for files created from *.cpp
    *.uo  for files created from *.cu
    *.ho  for files created from *.hip files

3) Move *.cpp and *.cu files into the rp/ directory

Stage VII: Unify and move the unit tests

1) Move unit tests to rp/tests/cpu and rp/tests/cuda

2) Remove rpc/ and rpg/ directories

   -> We should also figure out how to unify the unit tests, but have 
   not yet planned for this.


